The composer button that could only open an empty dialog
========================================================

The button in the composer wearing a pencil struck through is "Add MCP context". It is not a Linux
casualty and it is not a stub — but on this box it could not do anything, and that is worth removing
rather than shipping.

The picker browses `resources` and `prompts`, and it deliberately skips built-in adapters:

    const servers = list.filter(s => !s.builtin);

That filter is correct. Measured against all six built-ins in this build, both methods come back
`-32601 Method not found`:

    --workspace         resources ERR -32601   prompts ERR -32601
    --in-app-browser    resources ERR -32601   prompts ERR -32601
    --memory            resources ERR -32601   prompts ERR -32601
    --fetch             resources ERR -32601   prompts ERR -32601
    --computer-use      resources ERR -32601   prompts ERR -32601
    --browser-use       resources ERR -32601   prompts ERR -32601

So the dialog could only ever say "Configure an MCP server in Tools & skills to add external
context". A working control with nothing behind it, sitting in a row beside controls that work, and
next to a crossed-out looking glyph that reads as "disabled".

Two changes:

  * the button appears only when an external server exists to browse. The count comes from
    `getLocalMcpServers` filtered on `!builtin`, is cached, and is invalidated when the server list
    is edited. On a build with only the built-ins it is not in the composer at all.
  * its glyph is a link rather than a struck-through pencil, because "attach something from outside"
    is what it does, and the old one advertised the opposite.

